    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Foreign Relations: Regional Diplomacy and Major Powers",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iran’s diplomatic institutions, regional engagement, China and Russia ties, and disputes involving Europe and sanctions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-foreign-relations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Relationships operate on several levels",
        "content": "Iran’s external relationships cannot be understood through a single label such as isolation or alignment. The inspected record contains regional diplomatic engagement, coordination with China and Russia, and continuing disputes involving European governments. These tracks concern different interests and can proceed simultaneously. A declaration of cooperation does not establish a defense guarantee, just as a diplomatic opening does not settle every regional disagreement. [s2][s3][s4]\n\nThis overview introduces those distinctions through documented examples. It is not a complete account of conditions at the editorial cutoff: subsequent developments require further verification before publication. The country and regional pages in this branch provide the intended structure for deeper treatment of neighboring states, China, Russia, Europe, Central Asia, and economic diplomacy.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "formal-security-authority",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who coordinates security policy?",
        "content": "Iran’s constitutional framework places national-security coordination across several institutions. Article 176 provides for a Supreme National Security Council chaired by the president. Its responsibilities include formulating defense and national-security policies within the general policies determined by the Leader and coordinating relevant political, intelligence, social, cultural, and economic activities. Council decisions become effective after the Leader’s confirmation. [s1]\n\nThis establishes why the presidency alone is an incomplete guide to formal security authority. It does not reveal how a particular negotiation was conducted, which participants exercised the greatest influence, or how disagreements were resolved in practice. Those questions require evidence tied to the specific episode rather than inference from an organizational chart. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-engagement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional diplomacy and the limits of rapprochement",
        "content": "A useful example of regional engagement is the announced agreement to resume Saudi-Iranian diplomatic relations. The European External Action Service welcomed the announcement in March 2023 and described implementation as a prospective contribution to regional stability. Its statement is evidence of the EU’s response and expectations, rather than proof that the agreement resolved regional conflicts. [s2]\n\nThe European Council on Foreign Relations subsequently analyzed the rapprochement as a potential mechanism for managing tensions. Its policy argument favored supporting regional dialogue while acknowledging exposure to wider crises. That interpretation helps distinguish diplomatic risk management from comprehensive reconciliation: maintaining a channel can be valuable even when substantive disagreements persist. It remains an analytical judgment, not a shared official assessment by all parties. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "china-russia",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Coordination with China and Russia",
        "content": "A joint statement issued after a Beijing meeting in March 2025 records coordination among China, Russia, and Iran on the nuclear issue and sanctions. The participants called for diplomatic engagement, opposed sanctions they characterized as unlawful and unilateral, and emphasized the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty and the work of the International Atomic Energy Agency, the UN-associated nuclear verification body. [s4]\n\nThe statement also records an intention to strengthen coordination through BRICS and the Shanghai Cooperation Organization. It establishes the governments’ stated positions and chosen diplomatic forums. It does not independently verify Iran’s nuclear compliance, demonstrate that sanctions were removed, or establish that the three governments have identical interests. Its language about peaceful nuclear purposes must therefore be attributed to the participants. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "europe-nuclear-disputes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Europe, the nuclear agreement, and sanctions",
        "content": "The Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action, usually abbreviated JCPOA, is the nuclear agreement concluded in 2015. A European Parliamentary Research Service briefing describes the overlapping international and European sanctions frameworks and the deterioration of diplomacy during 2025, including the complications introduced by Israeli and US strikes against Iran in June. The briefing is a dated account, not a substitute for checking subsequent legal measures. [s5]\n\nThe Council of the EU’s sanctions chronology records the reimposition of nuclear-related restrictive measures in September 2025. Its account represents the EU’s institutional position on the process. Assessing contested claims about the legality of that process requires examination of the relevant instruments and competing legal arguments; the chronology alone cannot settle them. [s6]\n\nFor international readers, the essential distinction is between a diplomatic agreement, verification of obligations, and legally operative restrictions. These are connected but separate subjects. A political statement about dialogue does not establish that a restriction has ended, and an older sanctions summary cannot reliably describe the rules at a later date. [s4][s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5", "s6"]
      }
